---
name: dev-task
version: 2.2.0
description: lead dev assignment for a planned dev bead in atm-bd-orchestration. The bead is the assignment; task_id is the bead id.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - top
  - worktree_path
  - branch
optional_variables:
  - lead
  - cc
  - test_command
defaults:
  lead: team-lead
  cc: team-lead
  test_command: "just validate"
metadata:
  type: dev-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}">
  <description><![CDATA[Implement dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (sprint {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}). `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is the assignment: its description, design, acceptance criteria and metadata are authoritative.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <cut-from><![CDATA[{{ top | string | cdata_escape }}]]></cut-from>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[`{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` was cut from `{{ top | string | cdata_escape }}`, the pushed top of the phase stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}`. Work only on this branch. Never edit or push another branch, and never wait for another layer's sanity check, QA or CI. Other dev beads run in parallel and the top moves while you work: before closing, perform the developer's one rebase onto the current pushed top and push. Before closing, the developer opens or locates the draft PR for this branch with base from the bead's `metadata.pr_target` and records its number and URL in the close vars. The lead stacks and links the layer only after its sanity check passes; you never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`, and never rebase after closing. Once linked the layer is frozen: every later finding against it is fixed on a new layer at the top of the stack, never here.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Claim the bead, then start the task. `BEADS_ACTOR` must equal your `ATM_IDENTITY` (claims and closes are recorded as that actor); if it is empty, pass `--actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` to every `bd` write. Check that the bead is ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json | jq -e --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} 'any(.[]; .id == $b)'`. When it prints `true`, run `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim`, then `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`, and begin executing in the same work session. Make reasonable assumptions and do not ask clarifying questions unless a real blocker prevents execution.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `false` the bead is not ready: do not claim it and do not start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (its open blockers), and `bd show <blocker>` for each (status, assignee, and what it is itself waiting on). Report it with `atm send {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`: the bead, why it is not ready, and which bead or agent has to move. Then wait; {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} re-dispatches or cancels the task.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` before coding. Treat it as the authoritative source for scope, deliverables, deletion targets, validation, and acceptance criteria; `metadata.owned_paths` is your file fence. If this assignment and the bead differ, the bead wins and the mismatch must be reported. Then read every id in `metadata.requirements` (in the documents `.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/manifest-get docs.requirements` lists) and `metadata.adrs` (in those of `manifest-get docs.architecture`) before coding: your change must satisfy each one, and QA checks it against them. `NONE` means the plan declares none; if the work turns out to touch a requirement or ADR anyway, report it to {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} before coding past it.]]></step>
    <step id="c0"><![CDATA[If this task touches Rust, read `.claude/skills/rust-development/guidelines.txt` and `.claude/skills/rust-best-practices/patterns/practice-inventory.md` before editing; the reviewers score against them.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Work from the bead's deliverables and acceptance criteria; nothing outside its scope. A private checklist outside the tracked tree is optional. Commit on `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` as items land and push early (`git push -u origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` first) so the work is never only local. A push is not a close and needs no report.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Finish every item. Re-read the bead's acceptance criteria and confirm each is fully met; do not weaken a test, skip a criterion, or leave a placeholder. Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and the bead's validation commands until they pass with zero failures.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[Before dev-complete, perform the developer's one rebase onto the current pushed top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}` (the `sc-gh-stack-view` skill (`gh-stack-view` until it is installed), the last open row; `{{ top | string | cdata_escape }}` if nothing was linked since you started): `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/<current top>`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, then `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Record the full head SHA and the branch you rebased onto. Never rebase after closing.]]></step>
    <step id="g"><![CDATA[As the last work step before closing, open or locate the branch PR with `gh api -X POST repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls` (head = `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`, base = `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json | jq -r '.[0].metadata.pr_target'`; a draft is allowed), then record its number and URL. Close the bead and task together: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "dev complete at <short sha>"` and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/dev-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/dev-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. Build vars from that template's required fields, including `pr_number` and `pr_url`, with `commit` the pushed head and `rebased_onto` the top from step f. Write the vars file outside the repository and never commit it. Only this close frees your queue: the next assignment stays queued and never starts until `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is closed, and a plain `atm send` report does not close it.{% if cc and cc != lead %} Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc | string | cdata_escape }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`.{% endif %}]]></step>
    <step id="g1"><![CDATA[If the bead cannot be completed at all, declare failure: `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status blocked --assignee "" --append-notes "failed: <reason>"` (never `bd close`: a closed dev bead releases its sanity check) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. A deliverable you could not finish is a failure, not a completion.]]></step>
    <step id="h"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
